<BaseLayout title="Pallet Conveyor Systems: CDLR, Chain, Roller Types &#x26; Load Capacity | PackagingConveyor.com" description="Pallet conveyor systems: CDLR, drag chain, roller, and turntable types. Load capacity, stretch wrap integration, and selection criteria for pallet handling." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Pallet Conveyor Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Pallet conveyors are heavy-duty conveyor systems that transport loaded and empty pallets through packaging, warehousing, and manufacturing operations. They use chain-driven live rollers (CDLR), drag chains, or heavy-duty powered rollers to move loads from 500 to 4,000+ pounds between palletizers, stretch wrappers, pallet labelers, staging areas, and shipping docks.</p>
<p>Pallet conveyors form the backbone of every end-of-line packaging system. They connect the palletizer output to the stretch wrapper, then route wrapped pallets to staging lanes or directly to truck loading positions.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Pallet Conveyor Types: CDLR, Drag Chain, Roller &amp; Turntable</h2>
<h3>Chain-Driven Live Roller (CDLR)</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> are the most common pallet conveyor type. Steel rollers mount in a heavy-gauge steel frame and connect through sprocket-and-chain drives powered by a single motor. The chain loop drives the rollers, which carry the pallet on their surface.</p>
<p>CDLR advantages include roller slip capability (the chain can continue driving while rollers stop under a stationary pallet, enabling accumulation), wide load capacity range (1,000-4,000 lbs standard), and compatibility with standard GMA pallets. Hytrol's Model 26-CRRC and Intelligrated's (Honeywell) pallet conveyor lines use CDLR construction as their standard platform.</p>
<p>Roller diameter determines load capacity and durability. Common sizes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>2.5-inch diameter rollers:</strong> light-duty pallet conveyors, empty pallet handling, loads up to 1,500 lbs</li>
<li><strong>3.5-inch diameter rollers:</strong> standard GMA pallet loads, 1,500-3,000 lbs, most common size</li>
<li><strong>5-inch diameter rollers:</strong> heavy-duty applications, 3,000-4,000+ lbs, drum handling, steel/building materials</li>
</ul>
<h3>Drag Chain Conveyors</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/">Drag chain</a> pallet conveyors use two or three strands of heavy-duty chain running in steel or UHMW tracks. Pallets ride directly on the chain strands. The chain is driven by sprockets at one end and idles on sprockets at the other end.</p>
<p>Drag chain suits non-standard pallet sizes, slave pallets, and applications where pallets are too damaged or irregular for reliable roller transport. Drag chain also handles heavier loads than CDLR because the load distributes across the full chain length rather than individual roller bearings. The disadvantage: drag chain does not support accumulation without separate zones, each with its own chain drive.</p>
<p>Dematic and TGW use drag chain conveyors for pallet handling in high-throughput warehouse and distribution systems where pallets transfer between AS/RS (Automated Storage and Retrieval Systems) and outbound staging.</p>
<h3>Heavy-Duty Powered Roller</h3>
<p>Heavy-duty powered roller conveyors use belt-driven or line-shaft-driven large-diameter rollers. They serve applications requiring higher speeds than CDLR or where chain maintenance is undesirable. Belt-driven roller conveyors generate less noise than chain-driven systems and require less lubrication.</p>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">Live roller conveyors</a> in heavy-duty configurations handle pallet loads using V-belt or round-belt drives between rollers. Load capacity depends on roller diameter, bearing rating, and belt tension.</p>
<h3>Turntable Conveyors</h3>
<p>Pallet turntables rotate pallets 90 or 180 degrees for direction changes in conveyor layouts. The turntable platform contains a short section of roller or chain conveyor that aligns with the incoming and outgoing conveyor lines. A motor or pneumatic actuator rotates the platform to the target angle.</p>
<p>Turntables handle direction changes where right-angle <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyors</a> cannot accommodate the pallet size or weight. Turntable cycle times range from 5-15 seconds per rotation, which limits throughput compared to continuous-motion right-angle transfers. Hytrol and Bastian Solutions manufacture standard pallet turntable units.</p>
<h3>Right-Angle Transfer (Chain Transfer)</h3>
<p>Chain transfer conveyors move pallets at 90-degree angles without rotation. Pop-up chain strands rise between the rollers of a CDLR conveyor, lift the pallet off the rollers, and carry it perpendicular to the original direction. Chain transfers handle higher throughput than turntables because they operate continuously without a rotation cycle.</p>
<p>Right-angle transfers serve as routing points in pallet conveyor systems, directing pallets to different staging lanes, dock doors, or storage rows based on PLC logic.</p>
<h2 id="load-heading">Pallet Conveyor Load Capacity and Sizing</h2>
<p>Pallet conveyor load capacity depends on roller diameter, roller spacing (pitch), bearing rating, chain size, and frame construction. Every component in the conveyor must carry the maximum expected load with a safety margin. Under-specifying any single component creates a weak link that limits the entire system.</p>
