<h3>Conveyor Capacity Classes</h3>
<p>Manufacturers categorize pallet conveyors into capacity classes based on maximum load rating:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Light duty (up to 1,500 lbs):</strong> Empty pallet handling, light consumer goods. Uses 2.5" diameter rollers on 6" pitch. Painted carbon steel frames.</li>
<li><strong>Standard duty (1,500-3,000 lbs):</strong> GMA pallets loaded with cases and cartons. Uses 3.5" diameter rollers on 4-6" pitch. Standard for food, beverage, and consumer goods packaging lines. Hytrol, Intelligrated, and Bastian Solutions all offer standard-duty CDLR platforms.</li>
<li><strong>Heavy duty (3,000-4,000+ lbs):</strong> Full beverage pallets, drums, building materials. Uses 5" diameter rollers on 3-4" pitch with heavy-gauge frames. Oversized bearings and #60 or #80 chain. Dematic and TGW build heavy-duty platforms for warehouse AS/RS integration.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Sizing Considerations</h3>
<p>Conveyor width must accommodate the pallet width plus clearance on each side. Standard clearance is 2-4 inches per side. For a 40-inch-wide GMA pallet, the conveyor frame width is typically 46-48 inches (between rails).</p>
<p>Conveyor length per section depends on pallet length plus spacing. A typical staging zone holds one pallet per position plus 6-12 inches of gap for sensor spacing and pallet separation.</p>
<h2 id="wrap-heading">Stretch Wrapper and Palletizer Integration</h2>
<p>Pallet conveyors integrate with palletizers and stretch wrappers at both ends of the end-of-line system. The conveyor design at each integration point must match the machine's cycle time, pallet orientation requirements, and elevation.</p>
<h3>Palletizer Discharge</h3>
<p>The conveyor at the palletizer discharge receives completed pallets and moves them toward the stretch wrapper. This conveyor must start and stop quickly to clear the palletizer output position before the next pallet pattern is complete. Standard palletizer discharge conveyors use CDLR or drag chain in a heavy-duty configuration.</p>
<p>The conveyor should include 2-3 pallet positions of staging between the palletizer and the stretch wrapper. This buffer prevents the palletizer from stopping if the stretch wrapper is mid-cycle when the next pallet is ready. Photo-eye sensors at each position signal pallet presence to the PLC.</p>
<h3>Stretch Wrapper Infeed and Outfeed</h3>
<p>Two stretch wrapper types require different conveyor integration approaches:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Turntable stretch wrappers:</strong> The pallet transfers from the conveyor onto a rotating turntable. The turntable spins the pallet while a film carriage applies stretch film. After wrapping, the pallet transfers back onto the outfeed conveyor. The infeed and outfeed conveyors align with the turntable and use chain transfers or pop-up chain to move pallets on and off the turntable.</li>
<li><strong>Rotary arm (straddle) stretch wrappers:</strong> The pallet remains stationary on the conveyor while a rotating arm orbits the pallet, applying film. No turntable transfer is needed. The pallet stays on the conveyor throughout the wrapping cycle. This simplifies conveyor integration and eliminates the turntable transfer cycle time.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Post-Wrap Handling</h3>
<p>After stretch wrapping, pallets typically pass through a pallet labeler (which applies shipping labels to one or more faces), then route to staging lanes or directly to shipping dock positions. <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">Transfer conveyors</a> and turntables route pallets to the correct lane based on order, destination, or truck assignment. The PLC reads barcode or RFID data to determine routing.</p>
<h2 id="accum-heading">Pallet Accumulation and Staging</h2>
<p>Pallet <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a> queue loaded pallets between processes. CDLR conveyors support accumulation because the rollers can stop under a stationary pallet while the chain continues to drive. The chain slips on the roller sprocket, allowing pallets to accumulate without back-driving upstream pallets.</p>
<p>Pallet accumulation conveyors divide into zones, each controlled by a photo-eye sensor. Each zone holds one pallet. The PLC releases pallets one zone at a time as downstream positions clear. This prevents pallet-to-pallet contact and allows orderly sequencing.</p>
<h3>Staging Lane Configuration</h3>
<p>High-throughput packaging operations use multiple parallel staging lanes fed by transfer conveyors. Each lane holds 3-10 pallets depending on available floor space and buffer time requirements. Pallets route to lanes by SKU, shipping destination, or truck assignment.</p>
<p>Staging lane lengths depend on throughput and truck loading cycle time. A staging system sized correctly accumulates enough pallets to fill a truck (typically 20-26 pallets) without requiring the palletizer to stop. The formula:</p>
<p class="formula"><strong>Total Staging Positions = Pallets/hr x Truck Loading Time (hr) x Safety Factor (1.2-1.5)</strong></p>
<h2 id="mfg-heading">How to Evaluate a Pallet Conveyor Supplier</h2>
<h3>Match the Supplier to the Application</h3>
<p>Some suppliers focus on standard catalog pallet conveyor modules, while others specialize in engineered-to-order systems with controls, robotics, and warehouse software integration. Decide early whether you need a hardware supplier, a system integrator, or a turnkey automation partner.</p>
<h3>Ask About Installed Base and Service Support</h3>
<p>Pallet conveyors are heavy-duty assets that often stay in service for decades. Ask how many similar pallet systems the supplier has commissioned, what service territory they cover, how quickly they can support a breakdown, and which spare parts are standard versus proprietary.</p>
<h3>Review the Controls Boundary</h3>
<p>The biggest project risk is often at the interface between mechanical conveyor, controls, and upstream or downstream equipment. Clarify who owns PLC logic, field devices, safety zoning, communication with palletizers or wrappers, and final commissioning responsibility.</p>
<h3>Compare on Total Lifecycle Fit</h3>
<p>Do not compare suppliers on quote price alone. Compare load rating margin, chain and bearing access, spare parts availability, maintenance labor, future expansion flexibility, and whether the design uses standard components your maintenance team can realistically support.</p>
<p>PackagingConveyor.com is not affiliated with any supplier. See our <a href="/about/">about page</a> for our editorial approach.</p>
